course: dictionary
lesson: 4396
title: One thing, many names
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A hairgrip is a small, thin metal clip used to hold hair in place. You can often work out what a word means from the words near it. Here, the sentence says people use hairgrips «to keep hair tidy for school, dance or sport», so a hairgrip must be something for hair.
explain: Some words change from place to place. In the US, a hairgrip is called a «bobby pin», and in Australia people may say «bobby pin» or «hair clip». They all mean the same small clip, so when you read a word you do not know, check whether the text uses a different name for it.
explain: Read the warning sentence carefully: «Never push anything like a pencil or hairgrip into your ear; it can hurt the eardrum, the thin skin deep inside the ear that lets you hear, so tell an adult if something is stuck.» The clues never, hurt and tell an adult show that this is a safety rule. Swap in your meaning to check it still makes sense.
fact: A hairgrip is a noun. You can have one hairgrip or many hairgrips.
fact: «Hairgrip» is a UK word. In the US it is called a «bobby pin».
fact: In Australia, people may say «bobby pin» or «hair clip» for the same thing.
fact: In PE, hair is often tied back with hairgrips for safety.
fact: The eardrum is the thin skin deep inside the ear that lets you hear. Never push a hairgrip into your ear, and tell an adult if something is stuck.
exercise: What is a hairgrip? | *A small, thin metal clip used to hold hair in place | A short piece of string for a parcel | A type of hat worn in the rain | A tool for fixing bicycles
exercise: «Hairgrip» is a UK word. What is the US name for it? | *A bobby pin | A hairband | A hair dryer | A ponytail
exercise: «Never push anything like a pencil or hairgrip into your ear.» What should you do if something is stuck in your ear? | Push it further in | Keep it in and forget about it | *Tell an adult
test: In PE, why are hairgrips often used? | To make a clicking noise | *To tie hair back for safety | To hold a pencil | To look fashionable
test: Which word means the thin skin deep inside the ear that lets you hear? | *Eardrum | Pencil | Hairgrip | Clip
exam: Ella's American cousin asks for a «bobby pin». What does she mean? | A bicycle light | A kind of hat for winter | *A small, thin metal clip used to hold hair in place | A pair of sports shoes
